"On the SPOT: Enhancing Speculative Decodingfor Diffusion Language Modelsvia Commit-Order Prediction (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543)\nciteturn41267view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22543\",\"lineno\":235}); Total lines: 1284\nL224: At speculative round $t$, let $\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(\\cdot)$ denote the most recently available target conditional at masked position $s$. These conditionals are stale: hypothetical future commitments change the true target distribution, but no additional target forward is performed while drafting. Our drafter is a three-layer transformer encoder ($d_{\\mathrm{model}}=128$, $8$ heads, $0.62$M parameters).\nL225: Each masked position is represented using its stale top-$32$ probabilities (raw and log), eight summary statistics, and two path features encoding the positions already committed along the hypothetical prefix and its depth. Given a hypothetical state $y$, the model predicts $q_{\\phi}(s\\mid y)$, where $s\\in\\mathcal{M}(y),$ a distribution over the target’s next committed position. The same prefix-conditional predictor is learned in Algorithm cite148†1 and recursively queried during Algorithm cite149†2 .\nL226: The drafter uses no target hidden states, token embeddings, or text, and can therefore be easily evaluated across different DLMs.\nL227: #### Training.\nL228: We train $q_{\\phi}$ entirely from draft-free target trajectories (Algorithm cite148†1 ). Let $(s_{t}^{\\star},s_{t+1}^{\\star},\\ldots)$ denote the target commit sequence following decoding state $x_{t}$. For each prefix depth $d$, the first $d$ commitments define a hypothetical state $y_{t,d}$ while the stale conditionals $\\widehat{p}_{t}$ remain fixed; the next target commitment $s_{t+d}^{\\star}$ is used as supervision.\nL229: This produces prefix-conditioned examples $(\\widehat{p}_{t},y_{t,d},s_{t+d}^{\\star})$ that match the states encountered during recursive drafting. We optimize $\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{order}}(\\phi)=-\\mathbb{E}_{(\\widehat{p},y,s^{\\star})\\sim\\mathcal{E}}\\log q_{\\phi}(s^{\\star}\\mid y),$ where $\\mathcal{E}$ is the resulting training set. Full data-generation and optimization details are given in Appendix cite68†D.1 .\nL230: #### Inference.\nL231: At inference (Algorithm cite149†2 ), drafting starts from the current target state $x_{t}$ and stale conditionals $\\widehat{p}_{t}$. For every candidate prefix $y$, the order model scores the remaining positions using $q_{\\phi}(\\cdot\\mid y)$, and each position is paired with token hypotheses from the stale target conditional. Let $\\mathcal{H}_{t}(s)$ denote the token-hypothesis set for position $s$.\nL232: At $T=0$, its primary hypothesis is $\\widetilde{v}_{0}(s)=\\argmax_{v}\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(v),$ with the runner-up additionally included when the stale top-$1$ probability is below $0.6$. At $T>0$, $\\mathcal{H}_{t}(s)$ uses the coupled stale draw of Eq. cite150†4 , introduced formally in Section cite21†4.3 .\nL233: For a candidate child $z=y\\oplus(s,v)$, define its prefix mass recursively as\nL234: \nL235:  | $$m(z)=m(y)\\,q_{\\phi}(s\\mid y)\\,\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(v),\\qquad m(x_{t})=1.$$  |  | (3)\nL236: Algorithm cite149†2 uses DDTree, a level-batched best-first search adapted from cite139†Ringel and Romano (2026) , to allocate budget $D$ to the highest-mass candidate states. Since every factor in Eq. cite151†3 lies in $[0,1]$, a child’s mass cannot exceed its parent’s, allowing selected nodes to be expanded level-by-level while recovering the same top-$D$ fixpoint as sequential best-first search. A calibration temperature $\\tau$ is applied to $q_{\\phi}$ before search; the final model uses $\\tau=1$.\nL237: #### Adaptive budget and padding.\nL238: Algorithm cite149†2 treats $D$ as a hard upper bound. We additionally apply a prefix-mass floor $\\gamma$, discarding candidates with $m(z)<\\gamma$ and thereby adapting the realized verification budget to drafter confidence. Independently, the target batch may be padded with dummy replicas to maintain a fixed $(1+D)L$ shape and reduce batch-shape-dependent numerical variation.\nL239: We report the padded $\\gamma=0$ configuration as SPOT and the unpadded configuration with a floor calibrated once per target ($\\gamma=0.1$ on SDAR-8B; Appendix cite73†D.5 ) as SPOT-$\\gamma$. The complete budget, mass-floor, and padding ablations are given in Section cite30†5.5 .\nL240: ### 4.3 Diffusion-block verification by coupled-noise match\nL241: \nL242: #### Greedy verification.\nL243: \nL244: At $T=0$, the target transition $\\Phi_{0}$ in Eq. cite152†1 is deterministic. Therefore, a drafted edge $(y,z)$ is accepted exactly when $z=\\Phi_{0}(y)$. Proposition cite153†1 is elementary and included both for completeness and as a basis for the proof of Proposition cite154†2 .\nL245: ###### Proposition 1 (Greedy exactness).\nL246: \nL247: For any drafter $\\mathcal{D}$ and any finite chain or tree draft, state-equality verification at $T=0$ produces exactly the draft-free target trajectory: $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{0,\\mathcal{D}}=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{0}.$\nL248: #### Sampling with shared Gumbel noise.\nL249: At $T>0$, state equality cannot use independent drafter and verifier samples: even identical distributions would then produce different tokens with nonzero probability. Instead, we couple their sampling through shared randomness. For every absolute output position $a$ and vocabulary item $v$, draw $G_{a,v}\\overset{\\mathrm{iid}}{\\sim}\\mathrm{Gumbel}(0,1),$ and let $G=\\{G_{a,v}\\}_{a,v}$ denote the resulting table.\nL250: The table is shared by drafter and verifier and indexed by absolute position, so the same position uses the same Gumbel row in both.\nL251: For target state $x$, the target draw is $v^{\\star}_{G}(x)=\\argmax_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\left[\\frac{1}{T}\\log p_{\\theta,s^{\\star}(x)}(v\\mid x,C)+G_{a(s^{\\star}(x)),v}\\right]$, which defines the transition $\\Phi_{G}(x)=x\\oplus\\bigl(s^{\\star}(x),v^{\\star}_{G}(x)\\bigr).$ By the Gumbel–argmax identity, $v^{\\star}_{G}(x)\\sim\\pi_{T,s^{\\star}(x)}(\\cdot\\mid x,C)$. The drafter applies the same noise to its stale conditional:\nL252:  | $$\\widetilde{v}_{G}(s)=\\argmax_{v\\in\\mathcal{V}}\\left[\\frac{1}{T}\\log\\widehat{p}_{t,s}(v)+G_{a(s),v}\\right].$$  |  | (4)\nL253: \nL254: Thus, whenever the stale and fresh conditionals have the same Gumbel-perturbed argmax, drafter and target propose exactly the same token. Conditioned on $G$, $\\Phi_{G}$ is deterministic, so verification again reduces to state equality: $z=\\Phi_{G}(y).$\nL255: ###### Proposition 2 (Coupled-noise exactness).\nL256: \nL257: For any drafter $\\mathcal{D}$ and $T>0$, the Gumbel-driven draft-free trajectory satisfies $X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(G)\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$, and for every realization $g$ of $G$, $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}(g)=X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}(g)$. Consequently, $X^{\\mathrm{Spec}}_{T,\\mathcal{D}}\\overset{d}{=}X^{\\mathrm{DF}}_{T}$.\nL258: Propositions cite153†1 and cite154†2 therefore separate correctness from draft quality: the verifier always preserves the target trajectory or distribution, while the drafter determines only how many future target transitions are already present in the tree and can be accepted in one target forward. The complete formal setup and proofs are given in Appendix cite34†A . For a drafted position $s$, let $\\widehat{\\pi}_{T,s}$ and $\\pi_{T,s}$ denote the stale and fresh temperature-adjusted conditionals.\nL259: The shared-Gumbel coupling of cite141†Daliri et al. (2025) satisfies $\\Pr[\\widetilde{v}_{G}(s)=v^{\\star}_{G}(s)]\\geq\\frac{1-\\delta_{T}}{1+\\delta_{T}},$ where $\\delta_{T}=D_{\\mathrm{TV}}\\!\\left(\\widehat{\\pi}_{T,s},\\pi_{T,s}\\right).$ Hence coupled verification becomes more effective as the stale and fresh sampling distributions become closer.\nL260: #### Relation to rejection sampling.\nL261: When the drafter exposes an explicit sequential proposal distribution, exact verification can alternatively be performed by rejection sampling. Appendix cite51†B formalizes this alternative, proves its exactness and maximal per-link acceptance probability $1-D_{\\mathrm{TV}}(\\pi_{k},q_{k})$, and validates its implementation. Classical per-link RS assumes a sequential proposal interface, whereas coupled-noise match requires only state equality and therefore applies directly to our batched draft trees.\nL262: ## 5 Experiments\nL263: \nL264: We establish that SPOT takes fewer target forwards with higher throughput than every baseline at $T{=}0$ and $T{=}1$ (Section cite27†5.2 and Section cite28†5.3 ), then a checkpoint transfers across targets without additional training (Section cite29†5.4 ), and finally each component of the drafter earns its place (Section cite30†5.5 ).\nL265: ### 5.1 Experimental setup\nL266: Our headline target is SDAR-8B (cite119†Cheng et al., 2026 ), a dense block-causal diffusion LM at block width $L{=}32$ with its exact prefix KV cache. The same recipe gives drafters for SDAR-4B and SDAR-1.7B and for the mixture-of-experts LLaDA2.0-mini (cite147†Bie et al., 2025 ), and transfer is measured on two further targets, LLaDA-8B (cite117†Nie et al., 2025 ) and Dream-7B (cite120†Ye et al., 2025 ): six diffusion language models in all.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPredicting Privileged Information Effectiveness for On-Policy Self-Distillation (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1)\nciteturn41267view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22512v1\",\"lineno\":190}); Total lines: 1041\nL163: ##### Summary and Skill Hints.\nL164: \nL165: Summary hints condense a reference trajectory into a brief description of its solution [cite95†19 ]. Skill hints provide more detailed guidance by combining high-level strategies, mistake analysis, and reusable skills derived from the reference trajectory [cite101†26 ]. We also use the student model itself to generate both types of hints.\nL166: ##### Checklist Hints.\nL167: \nL168: We generate checklists from a successful reference and a failed student trajectory, listing task requirements for the teacher. Like rubric-conditioned self-distillation [cite102†20 , cite103†6 ], this format provides structured criteria rather than a target response.\nL169: ### 2.2 Offline Metrics\nL170: \nL171: Training a separate student for every PI candidate is computationally expensive. We therefore study offline metrics that can assess PI effectiveness before training and help identify what distinguishes PI that produces better learning signals for the student policy.\nL172: \nL173: Using the notation above, let $R(\\tau)\\in\\{0,1\\}$ denote whether trajectory $\\tau$ completes its task successfully. We define the following metrics:\nL174: ##### PI utility.\nL175: \nL176: PI utility measures the improvement in task success obtained by exposing the same model to PI:\nL177: \nL178:  | $$U(I)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}(\\cdot\\mid I)}[R(\\tau)]-\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}}[R(\\tau)].$$  |  | (2)\nL179: \nL180: A positive value indicates that the teacher solves more tasks when conditioned on PI, motivating the hypothesis that higher PI utility leads to more effective OPSD training.\nL181: ##### Sampled-token Reverse KL.\nL182: \nL183: For a student trajectory $\\tau=(y_{1},\\ldots,y_{L_{\\tau}})$, we estimate the reverse KL from the sampled-token log-ratio, using its absolute magnitude so that shifts in either direction count:\nL184: \nL185:  | $$d_{I}(\\tau)=\\frac{1}{L_{\\tau}}\\sum_{t=1}^{L_{\\tau}}\\left|\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}(y_{t}\\mid s_{t})}{\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}(y_{t}\\mid s_{t},I)}\\right|,\\qquad y_{t}\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}(\\cdot\\mid s_{t}).$$  |  | (3)\nL186: Intuitively, a larger reverse KL indicates that PI more strongly changes the teacher’s predictions on student-visited states, providing a stronger potential learning signal. We report $D(I)=\\mathbb{E}_{\\tau\\sim\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}}[d_{I}(\\tau)]$ over the sampled student trajectories.\nL187: ##### Error-step KL Contrast ($\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$).\nL188: \nL189: This metric measures whether PI suppresses tokens more strongly at identified errors than at comparable steps in other rollouts. An agent trajectory contains multiple interaction steps, each of which may contain several generated tokens. For step $j$, let $\\mathcal{T}_{j}(\\tau)\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,L_{\\tau}\\}$ denote its token indices and define\nL190:  | $$d^{-}_{I}(\\tau,j)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{T}_{j}(\\tau)|}\\sum_{t\\in\\mathcal{T}_{j}(\\tau)}\\left[\\log\\frac{\\pi_{\\theta}^{S}(y_{t}\\mid s_{t})}{\\pi_{\\theta}^{T}(y_{t}\\mid s_{t},I)}\\right]_{+},\\qquad[z]_{+}=\\max(z,0).$$  |  | (4)\nL191: \nL192: Because KL can vary with step position, we subtract a task- and position-matched baseline to reduce these effects. For each PI condition $I$, task $x$, and absolute step index $j$, the baseline is\nL193:  | $$b_{I}(x,j)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{R}_{x,j}|}\\sum_{\\tau^{\\prime}\\in\\mathcal{R}_{x,j}}d^{-}_{I}(\\tau^{\\prime},j),$$  |  | (5)\nL194: \nL195: where $\\mathcal{R}_{x,j}$ is the set of sampled trajectories for task $x$ that reach step $j$. For each trajectory, a judge labels the set $E_{\\tau}$ of error step indices. With $\\mathcal{E}_{F}=\\{(\\tau,j):R(\\tau)=0,\\ j\\in E_{\\tau}\\}$, we report\nL196:  | $$\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}(I)=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{E}_{F}|}\\sum_{(\\tau,j)\\in\\mathcal{E}_{F}}\\left[d^{-}_{I}(\\tau,j)-b_{I}(x_{\\tau},j)\\right],$$  |  | (6)\nL197: \nL198: where $\\mathcal{E}_{F}$ contains every flagged step from a failed trajectory, $x_{\\tau}$ is the task associated with $\\tau$, and each flagged step receives equal weight. Positive values indicate that PI targets identified errors more strongly than matched positions within the same task.\nL199: ### 2.3 Experimental Setup\nL200: \nL201: We evaluate OPSD on ALFWorld [cite108†24 ], AppWorld [cite109†25 ], and $\\tau^{2}$-retail [cite110†2 ], using Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507, Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507, and Qwen3-30B-A3B-Instruct-2507 as students. We implement OPSD in SkyRL [cite116†3 ] with the sampled-token reverse-KL objective and report pass@1 from the best three-checkpoint window.\nL202: Offline metrics are computed on training or development tasks disjoint from the evaluation sets used to measure post-training performance. Appendices cite52†B and cite60†C provide the benchmark splits, sampling protocols, training hyperparameters, and evaluation settings.\nL203: ## 3 Results and Analysis\nL204: ### 3.1 Which PI Formats Work Best?\nL205: \nL206: Figure cite117†2 compares PI formats (top), generation sources (middle), and self-generated hints (bottom) across three benchmarks and student models.\nL207: \nL208: Figure 2: OPSD improvement relative to baseline across PI formats and sources; columns correspond to ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. (a)–(c) Mean performance across GLM and Qwen sources. (d)–(f) GLM minus Qwen pass@1; positive values favor GLM. (g)–(i) Summary and Skill gains for GLM, Qwen, and Self.\nL209: \nL210: (a)\nL211: \nL212: (b)\nL213: (c)\nL214: \nL215: (d)\nL216: \nL217: (e)\nL218: \nL219: (f)\nL220: \nL221: (g)\nL222: \nL223: (h)\nL224: \nL225: (i)\nL226: ##### Trajectory formats generally outperform hints, but detailed PI can hurt already-capable models.\nL227: Trajectory PI generally outperforms compressed hints (Figures cite118†2(a) –cite119†2(c) ). Instruct models benefit further when trajectories include observations and teacher thinking tokens, suggesting that additional context strengthens the training signal. However, richer PI is not universally beneficial for the thinking model: adding teacher thinking tokens to Action $+$ Obs.\nL228: hurts its performance on ALFWorld and AppWorld, while on $\\tau^{2}$, adding either observations or thinking tokens causes even larger declines in Figure cite119†2(c) . Its high pass@4 of $88.9$ on $\\tau^{2}$ suggests that it already reasons effectively, leaving additional trajectory context less room to help and more potential to interfere with its existing strategy.\nL229: ##### Effective PI provides context beyond the correct solution.\nL230: Skills, which augment the solution with mistake analysis and reusable strategies, are often the strongest hint format. They lead for all three students on ALFWorld, both 4B students on AppWorld, and 4B-Instruct on $\\tau^{2}$.\nL231: Together with the gains from adding environment observations to trajectories, these results suggest that effective PI should do more than reveal or summarize a correct solution: it should provide context that helps the student understand its mistakes and how to act successfully in the environment.\nL232: ##### The PI source matters, especially when generating hints.\nL233: Figures cite120†2(d) –cite121†2(f) show that GLM-5.2 PI almost always outperforms Qwen3.7-Plus PI: for 4B-Instruct, GLM wins 20 of 21 comparisons, and its lead reaches 36.0 points on ALFWorld hints but stays within 3.2 points for trajectory formats. Source choice thus matters most when trajectories are rewritten into guidance: for trajectory PI, the source determines only the reference behavior the teacher sees, whereas for hints the generator also decides which information is kept and how it is expressed.\nL234: ##### Self-generated hints yield positive performance gains.\nL235: \nL236: For 4B-Instruct, both self-generated Summary and Skill hints improve performance on all three benchmarks (Figures cite122†2(g) –cite123†2(i) ). These results suggest that the student can act as its own hint generator given the reference solution and still benefit from OPSD.\nL237: ### 3.2 What Do PI Metrics Reveal?\nL238: Given the variation across PI configurations above and the substantial cost of training each one, we next ask whether post-training effectiveness can be assessed without running OPSD for every candidate. Figure cite113†3 plots each PI metric against post-training performance for every student under each PI configuration across ALFWorld, AppWorld, and $\\tau^{2}$-retail. All diagnostics are computed on training or development tasks disjoint from the post-training evaluation sets.\nL239: These comparisons show how utility, the distributional-shift statistic $D$, and the error-step contrast $\\Delta d^{-}_{\\mathrm{err}}$ relate to the performance gains produced by different PI configurations.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDon’t Retrain, Remix: Adaptation of Web Agents using Mixture Hypernetworks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1)\nciteturn41267view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22539v1\",\"lineno\":282}); Total lines: 600\nL272: Figure 3: KL anchor strength (RQ3). Success rate (%) on unseen TimeWarp domains with Qwen-3.5 4B as the anchor weight $\\beta$ of Eq. cite122†7 varies. The shaded column marks the best average, $\\beta=1.0$.\nL273: ##### RQ1: Transfer to unseen interfaces.\nL274: Tables cite123†1 and cite124†2 report transfer to U nseen domains and versions on TW and WAL, respectively. WebMix outperforms every baseline on both models and transfer types, often matching the performance of baselines trained on those interfaces. For example, it matches LoRA (in) on TW domains with both models. Gains are largest for domain transfer, at 7.5–8.3 percentage points (pp) over the best baseline on TW and 3.3–4.6pp on WAL.\nL275: Version transfer gains are smaller (1.8–3.2pp), and LoRA is a strong baseline there. Transfer is strongest for domains that are similar to the training domains. With Qwen-3.5, News gains 26pp over zero-shot, compared to $\\sim$5pp on Wiki and Shop. On WAL, OSS gains the most for both models (14–15pp). The one failure case is Map with Llama-3.1, where every unseen method scores zero. Composing adapters trained on other interfaces thus recovers most of the benefit of training on the target itself.\nL276: ##### RQ2: Retention on seen interfaces.\nL277: \nL278: WebMix’s transfer gains do not come at the cost of S een interface performance. In Tables cite123†1 and cite124†2 , WebMix is the best or tied-best method in every column. It improves over the strongest seen baseline, LoRA (all), by 4.6–6.3pp on TW domains, 1.8–2.4pp on TW versions, and 2.6–4.3pp on WAL. Training LoRA on all domains adds only 0.2–1.3pp over in-domain training, suggesting that WebMix’s gains do not solely come from more training data.\nL279: ##### RQ3: Design choices.\nL280: Hypernetwork design. Table cite125†3 isolates the contribution of each component. T2L Recons (Appendix cite47†B.2 ), which regresses the stored adapter updates, is the weakest variant. T2L SFT (Appendix cite48†B.3 ), which trains the same generator through the policy, improves on it by 9.17pp. Replacing weight generation with the mixture hypernetwork (§cite16†3.2 ) adds a further 3.75pp, mostly from News (+9pp).\nL281: Conditioning the router on the scout then adds another 4.58pp, for a total gain of 8.33pp over the strongest T2L variant. Composing a frozen bank is easier to learn from a handful of interfaces than generating weights. Appendix cite56†D.2 analyzes an idealization of T2L Recons. Scouting budget. Scout outperforms Mixture on average for every number of scouted tasks $n$, but $n{=}1$ is best. Larger $n$ gives a marginal gain on News (+1.5pp at $n{=}3$) but costs 6–8 points on Wiki, so we use $n{=}1$ throughout.\nL282: KL regularization. Figure cite126†3 varies the anchor weight $\\beta$. $\\beta{=}1.0$ is the best of the tested values on every domain and on average. Removing the KL term ($\\beta{=}0$) lowers the average by over 7pp. A stronger penalty ($\\beta{=}3.0$) also hurts. WebMix therefore benefits from moderate regularization.\nL283: ##### RQ4: Analysis.\nL284: \nL285: The domain adapters of the bank are nearly orthogonal (cosine below 0.16, Figure cite127†4(a) ), and without the KL anchor they are about twice as similar (Figure cite128†5 ). The router builds a held-out version from its nearest versions, e.g., v1 from v2 and v3 (Figure cite129†4(b) ). With v6 in the bank, it puts 96% of the weight on v6, so the mixture weights track interface similarity.\nL286: ## 5 Conclusion\nL287: Experience with one website makes the next one easier to use, and WebMix gives small, open-weight web agents the same advantage. A scout writes a short manual of the new interface, and a router, trained only on source interfaces, maps the encoded manual to mixing weights over a frozen adapter bank. The result is a standard LoRA adapter, produced without gradient updates on the target.\nL288: WebMix transfers to unseen domains and versions better than in-context learning and LoRA, often matches target-trained specialists. When the web changes, a web agent does not need retraining. It can simply remix what it already knows.\nL289: ## References\nL290:   * AI (2025) O. AI Computer using agent. Note: cite130†https://openai.com/index/computer-using-agent/†openai.com [Accessed 20-09-2026] Cited by: cite131†§1 .\nL291:   * Anthropic (2024) Anthropic Introducing computer use, a new claude 3.5 sonnet, and claude 3.5 haiku. Note: cite132†https://www.anthropic.com/news/3-5-models-and-computer-use†www.anthropic.com [Accessed 20-09-2026] Cited by: cite131†§1 .\nL292:   * Bai et al. (2026) H. Bai, D. Wang, L. Chen, B. Hao, P. Shao, Y. Yang, Y. He, and C. Zhuang StressWeb: a diagnostic benchmark for web agent robustness under realistic interaction variability. External Links: 2604.16385, cite133†Link Cited by: cite134†§2 .\nL293